diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. End-to-end connectivity between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) is healthy: bidirectional pings succeed with 0% loss and sub‑2 ms RTTs. The inter-AS “WAN” link between router1 and router2 is up at Layer 1/2: eth0 on both routers reports “Link detected: yes” at 10 Gbps, full duplex. At Layer 3/4, the eBGP session is established on both sides: router1 (AS 1, 193.10.11.1) shows neighbor 193.10.11.2 (AS 2) in Established state with prefixes received; router2 mirrors this, and each router’s routing table contains the BGP-learned route to the opposite LAN (router1 has 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 has 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). This explains why traffic flows normally despite the report.

Localization: No component appears degraded. The suspected link router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0 is operational; FRR bgpd on both routers is exchanging routes correctly; host interfaces are functioning.

Root cause: No current fault found; the reported outage is not observable now and may have been transient or user-local.

Corrective fix: No interface bounces or restarts are warranted. Do not restart any device. Recommend monitoring for flaps (BGP/links) and, if intermittent issues recur, capture bgpd logs and interface counters to correlate events. As of now, the network is healthy.
